French court rejects application to dismantle shops from Jungle refugee camp
A French tribunal has dismissed an application by Calais authorities to dismantle dozens of ad hoc shops and restaurants from the Jungle camp, home to thousands of refugees.
At a court hearing this week, Calais officials argued that the state should be able to dismantle a total of 72 temporary shops in which people within the camp sell goods from cigarettes to energy drinks, provide a haircut or sell tea for 50 cents (40 cups), or sell simplistic food for a few euros in temporary restaurants.
Calais's prefect agency justified the need to dismantle the sites because they represent taxes on an unpaused co-mortgage-administrative economy and because they pose a risk of fire and sanitation.
Humanitarian groups have told the tribunal that shops and restaurants are vital, to say free meals provided by a state-sponsored organization and other groups don't provide enough food for the increasing number of people in the camp.
They argued that temporary shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free meals for those who need them.
A tribunal in Lille ordered that there are no legal grounds for evicting people who run a temporary transaction, but said the concerns of the Calais Prefect "was perfectly understandable."
Judge Jean-Francheois Molla said food stores, cafes and restaurants played a much greater role than feeding people living in "extremity-premnant conditions."
He said the facilities provided peaceful rendezvous facilities for refugees and volunteers.
One of the places under threat of demolition was the Jungle Book Kids' Cafe of the camp, whose operations are non-prowisperable and offer 200 meals daily, English and french classes, as well as asylum advice to hundreds of vulnerable, lone underage minors in the camp.
More than 170,100,000 people have signed a petition to protect a children's coffee shop.
A letter sent to the Guardian stated that shutting down a coffee-affecting "threaten would be a catastrophe for these children, some of which are only eight years of age."
Two philanthropic organizations, Help Refugees and L'Auberge des Migrants, estimated on Friday that the number of people at the Jungle camp has risen to ninth to 9,000 people at a high point - since the number of arrivals rising sharply over the summer.
The price of food in Finland is falling clearly faster than the EU average
In Finland, food is getting sharply cheaper to the enjoyment of consumers and the chagrin of producers.
Food prices in Finland have fallen by nearly five per cent over three years, compared to a 0.9 per cent in EU countries.
At the same time, food in Germany and Sweden has become 1% more expensive.
Over the past three-period years, the price of food in Finland has fallen most among EU Member States that have the same level of wages as in Finland.
In comparable countries in the United Kingdom, the price of food has fallen faster than in Finland.
The information comes from a writing by Ilkka Lehtinen, Head of Development at Statistics Finland, which was published in the Tieto & Trends blog.
Lehtinen compared real prices, i.e. prices proportionate to the overall development of consumer prices.
The latest data are from June of this year.
At the beginning of the 2010s, food prices increased sharply.
In 2013, prices came close to the price level of 1994.
It was an alarming situation.
"We came close to losing the price advantage that came with EU membership," Lehtinen says.
Then a turnaround ensued, after which food has become cheaper every year from year to year.
The price is now weighed down by Russia's bans on food imports, which have swerved food exports from Finland to Russia.
That’s why there has been an increase in the supply of milk and meat products on the Finnish market and agricultural producer prices have declined.
"Putin's policies have helped the Finnish consumers in the short term," Lehtinen says.
Intensified prices of industry and commerce have also contributed to lower prices.
Large retail chains S Group and Kesko have both lowered their prices.
For the consumer, price competition has been reflected in spectacular promotional promotional campaigns.
However, Finland is still not a place of low-principal nutrition, even though the price of food has fallen extensively.
In 2015, food in Finland was among the fourth most expensive EU countries with a similar wage level to Finland.
According to Lehtinen, the price of food sold in Finland is significantly influenced by value added tax.
He found that if control countries had the same rate of value-to-equipment, the third cheapest food would be sold in Finland.
Lehtinen compared Finland to Denmark, Sweden, Austria, France, Italy, Britain, Germany and the Netherlands.
If the value-added tax rate was 7 per cent in the comparator countries, only German and Dutch would sell cheaper food than Finns.
"If we want to reach the level of old EU countries at the price of food, VAT must halve the average level of EU Member States and continue price competition," Lehtinen writes.
Mr Lehtinen points out that his calculations by VAT rates are indicative.
