Pamela Tola Receives Giant Compensation for Mould House
Actress Pamela Tola and her ex-husband Pauli Waroma, Managing Director Pauli Waroma, have won a serious dispute about an apartment they bought in Espoo in 2009.
The purchase price of a two-storey detached house had amounted to €333,000.
However, after the transaction, a serious moisture problem was identified in the house, causing microbial growth that is dangerous to health.
The couple were forced to sell the apartment in demolition condition in 2014.
The selling price had dropped to EUR 132,000, so a financial loss for Tola and Waroma amounted to as much as EUR 198,000.
The couple took the matter to court and demanded a price reduction from the seller there.
Seller disputed the claim, but Tola and Waroma defeated the case in both the District Court and the Court of Appeal.
The Court of Appeal handed a verdict in the case on Wednesday.
It upheld the district court's verdict, ordering the seller to pay Tola and Waroma a price reduction of EUR 150,000.
In addition, the salesperson will have to reimburse a total of EUR 47,344 for the couple’s legal and settlement costs.
The court sanctuated the price reduction a bit because some need to repair an apartment had already been known by buyers when doing a home sale.
According to the European Court of Justice of the Court of Appeal, however, the reported construction errors were materially more serious than what had been suggested.
Tola was looking for a good-reaching home
Tola told the Court of Appeal that the couple had specifically searched for a good-quality home because they didn't want to engage in reparations.
There was a perception for him that the building has been renovated and in good condition and that there was no inconvenience.
The condition inspection report cited as risks and impulses of ventilation and the insufficient management of rain and meltwater pouring along the rock surface.
However, it did not come to light from the discussions that there was an urgent issue.
Tola and Waroma took the view that rainwater from the cliff should perhaps be steered slightly within a couple of years.
The transactions took place in October 2009.
Within a couple of years it became apparent that moisture problems in the apartment were far more serious than thought.
Inspections carried out in 2012 and 2013 identified microbial growth in the building, according to Witnesses, due to rock rainwater as well as capillary geostimming moisture.
On the western side of the house, microbial-contaminated air could also get into the interior through the porch through the porch.
Mould or yeast that would enable a healthy-threatening risk with limit values were found in charitable samples from the utility room, basement staircase, living room window spree, as well as a basement washing room.
Terraces were built without permits
There was also controversy in the court over whether the apartment had been expanded in violation of a building permit.
The court ruled that Tola and Waroma were unaware of the City of Espoo’s decision, according to which changes had been made to the apartment in violation of a permit.
The terraces, among other things, had been built, even though there were no appropriate permits yet.
Mould problems, however, were a key issue in court.
According to the Housing Court of First Court of Auditors, on the basis of the information they had received from the seller prior to the transaction, assumed that the building was suitable for housing as such and that there was no need for extensive repairs in the near future.
According to the Court of Appeal, Tola and Waroma had no particular reason to carry out a larger-than-usual inspection.
Buyers have failed to comply with their reporting obligation, the Court of Appeal states in its judgment.
Huge archive discovery: hundreds of thousands of women wanted to give birth to children for Nazis - Finns in the register
Journalist Christopher Andersson, in a recent Dagens Nyheter article, writes about documents on micro-retects in the National Archives in Maryland of the U.S. about 218,1,500 women, all of whom wanted to marry SS men.
The majority of the women are German, but there are also U.S.-French, Dutch and Russian.
At least 65 Danes, 86 Norwegian and 14 Finns and 31 Sweatmen wanted Nazi brides.
The documents and registration cards come from a race agency operating in Nazi Germany.
The Race Agency operated under SS Director Heinrich Himmler.
In Nazi Germany, not a single SS man was allowed to have any children until he was examined by an SS doctor.
The same was true of even greater numbers for women who were supposed to be able to give birth to more than 10 children.
Andersson said gynaecological examinations were carried out in women to investigate.
The Racial Education Agency surveyed women's backgrounds by, among other things, exploring church books.
The idea was to make sure there weren't any Jews in women's genitals.
Women also had to be ready to relinquish their Swedish citizenship, as well as handwrinkled biographies and good testimonials from loved ones.
Racial agency wanted assurances that the women were "muscmerical", "saving," and "good National Socialists" who knew German well.
Women who didn't meet the requirements weren't approved as a bridal for SS men.
The article does not tell us what happened to 14 Finnish women found in archive documents.
SS leaders created a state-sponsored program, known as Lebensborn in 1936.
The program, among other things, mandated that each SS-husband should have four children, either in or out of a marriage.
Lebensborn homes look after children born out of wedlock and their mothers.
However, the program was not promoted very actively, the U.S. Holocaust Museum website says.
On the other hand, Nazi population politics focused on family and marriage.
The government encouraged getting married by offering loans and subsidies for receiving children.
An honorary racgraph of a German mother was awarded to women who had received at least four children.
In Natsi-Germany, marriages between "Arists" and "non-Eartainers" were prohibited by law, the museum's website states.
