MTK Hakkarainen on forest gift deprivation: More than 80 per cent of forest owners outside
The forest gift deduction is one tax change in the finance ministry's budget proposal for next year.
A tax deprecipitation is proposed in the Incomes Tax Act, which would be granted based on the gift tax of the generational change of forest space.
According to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the forest gift depreciation has four objectives: to promote generational changes in forest holdings, rejuvenating the age structure of forest owners, increase forest space size and increase the supply of wood.
The ministry's goal is to promote entrepreneurship-like forestry.
A forest-owner would make a depreciation of clean capital income from forestry.
In general, the idea of making such a tool to promote generational changes in forest holdings is good, comments Juha Hakkarainen, Forest Director of the Confederation of Finnish Agricultural Producers of Agricultural Producers and Forest Owners (MTK).
Hakkarainen, however, finds something to be criticised in a tax change bill.
Depreciation of a forest gift reduction is proposed for the clean capital income of the forest owner.
It's capital income minus all other deductions.
According to MTK and Hakkarainen, the effectiveness of the tax depreciation would be greater if the depreciation could be deducted directly from wood sales.
The Ministry of Social Affairs and Economic Affairs (MMED) starts with a depreciation of the pure capital income of forestry to prevent a loss of capital income from forestry, which should be deducted in other taxation.
Such a person would receive a tax benefit even if they don't sell a tree.
The calculation is unnecessarily complex.
An average forest owner is unable to perceive it, Hakkarainen says.
In addition, according to him, the model only applies to a small number of forest owners.
The tax deprecipitation limit tied to the yield value of forests is EUR 40,000 in the proposal.
It's been dragged too high, according to Hakkarainen.
According to him, as such, 82 per cent of Finnish forest owners will be removed from the scope of the depreciation.
The model would have an impact on about 10 per cent of the forest ownership community, he says.
The M.T.C. considers that the limit guarantees the goal of increasing space size.
According to Hakkarainen, forest gift depreciation has been reduced for decades.
It is driven by an aggravation of forest owners.
According to MTK, the average age of forest owners is currently more than 60 years, and about half of forest owners are pensioners.
