The word “trampoline” comes from the Spanish word el trampol, which means stepping stone.
On a giant trampoline, goal-seekers of the championship are jumping up to nearly 30-floor height, and must perform twielding sequences of motions (switches, folds and volts) and eventually land on their feet.
According to an official Olympic program, they will be judged based on implementation, difficulty and "flight time."
Thirty-two athletes from 17 countries, 16 men and 16 women will compete in Rio.
The most common direction of the Olympics is straight ahead.
