'Generation Demonstrates' in Ethiopia, a long-time U.S. all-time all-around
Violent Protest in Ethiopia
Providers calling for a political shift in Ethiopia have faced violent opposition from the government.
Witnesses say dozens of demonstrators have been gunned to death in clashes with police.
Is Ethiopia Declining?
Over the past decade, it has been one of Africa's most stable nations, a firm ally of the West whose economy is growing rapidly.
Over the past few months, however, anti-government protests have shattered the country as it has spread to an increasing number of areas.
Last week alone, thousands of people rushed to the streets calling for a fundamental political change.
According to human rights groups, the government's response was relentless.
Witnesses said officers fired and killed dozens of non-aggregated demonstrators.
Distributing videos recorded at demonstrations, believed to be at the end of last year or at the beginning of this year, will show that security officials are beating young people at the same time they were forcing them to stand with their hands against the wall.
The United Nations chief human rights official is now calling for an overcrowded investigation.
"Wasing this country together was always tricky, and in the future it's going to be even trickier," said Rashid Abdi, project manager for the Horn of Africa at the International Crisis Group.
Ethiopia is Africa's second most populous country after Nigeria, and Western countries appreciate its stability.
American military and reconnaissance services are working closely with Ethiopians to combat terrorist threats in the region, particularly in Somalia, and hardly any African country receives so much Western help.
Ethiopia's economy has been growing at an impressive momentum.
It's an extremely much improvement in its infrastructure, and there's even a new commuting train at Addis Ababa.
The city's streets are typically quiet, safe and clean.
However, although Ethiopia has not been a model image of democracy. Human rights groups have constantly talked about government repression, the country's opposition has been constrained and dissidents have been effectively silenced.
Many have been expelled, imprisoned, slayed or chased far into the desert.
But this could be about to change.
"If you smother people and don't have any other choice but to demonstrate, this is going to happen," Seyoum Teshome, a university teacher from Central Ethiopia, said.
The entire youth is demonstrating.
One generation is protesting.
Complaints are manifold and cover everything from land-based coalition to strrang strangulation.
After last year's much-criticised elections, the ruling party and its allies were given the last seat held by the opposition, and now it holds all seats in parliament.
At the same time, tensions along the border with Eritrea are mounting. The battle along this tense and controversial border required hundreds of lives in June.
Analysts fear separatist groups such as Oromo Liberation Front or Ogaden National Liberation Front, which have more or less defeated over the past few years, can try to exploit the turmoil and re-emerge.
Numerous perpetrators explain why, after years of turmoil, bitter emotions are now exploding.
The first appears to be seemingly harmworthy: smartphones.
Only over the past few years, a large number of Ethiopians have been able to communicate through social media as cheaper smartphones have become more common and the internet service has improved.
Even if the government shuts down access to Facebook and Twitter, as it often does, especially during demonstrations, many people will still be able to communicate the location through hidden online proxy servers.
A number of young Ethiopians said this is how they gathered for demonstrations.
Second, Ethiopia's two largest ethnic ethnic groups, the Oromos and Amweavers, will get along better.
Oromos and Amweavers aren't natural allies.
For a very long period of time, the Amls coming from Ethiopia's largely Christian highlands were successful in politics and business, taking advantage of oromics, many of whom are Muslims and who live in lowlands.
But even this is about to change.
"We're on our way to organizing under one umbrella," Oromo Director Mulatu Gemechu said.
The biggest demonstrations have taken place in Amhara and Oromo areas.
A lot of Amhara and Oromo think that Ethiopia is dominated terribly by tigre folklore. They cover about six per cent of the population and dominate armed forces, intelligence services, trade and politics.
A third cause of unequisolation is Meles Zenawi loss.
Meles, an ex-reaspiratory leader, was Ethiopia's prime minister for 17 years until he died of a secret illness in 2012.
He was considered a tactical genius, a man who saw around the corners.
Analysts say he was particularly keen to observe early signs of dissatisfaction and use secret envoys to make opponents untrue.
"Current governance doesn't have that kind of basic indignation," Conflict analyst Abdi said.
Ethiopia's new prime minister, Hailemariam Desalegn, was plplplucked relatively unfamiliar to fill Meles boots.
Contrary to Meles, originally from the tigre region of northern Ethiopia, Hailemariam is from the south.
Analysts say tiger-dominated security services don't trust him.
A lot of people fear this could lead to increasing bloodshed.
It was the last time Ethiopia had such a turmoil in 2005 when thousands of people protested about elections that analysts say it seemed that the government first fluctuated and plundered them.
The ensuing glimpser killed many protesters, albeit fewer than in the last few months, and that period of unease passed relatively quickly.
Development experts have lauded Ethiopian leaders for visionary infrastructure planning, such as a new commuting train, and significant advances in the fight against poverty.
But clearly, this has not halted the intensification of contempt by the internal Ethiopian government.
And now it's starting to get a dangerous, ethnic form.
Last month demonstrators in the amhara town of Ahardar attacked companies owned by ticers, and anti- tigre anger is becoming increasingly common on social media.
Analysts say demonstrations are putting the U.S. and other Western allies in a clumsy position.
The American government has used Ethiopia as a base for drone-hunting flights to neighboring Somali, even though it recently said it shutting down this base.
Although the Western countries clearly want to support democracy, they also do not want their already volatiliated allies in the region to collapse.
"This," Abdi said, "is a real recalcitation."
