According to NASA's new climatic model, Venus was likely to rehabilitate once.
Scientists at NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies developed a model to study Venus' past using tools and algorithms similar to those used by climate scientists.
The model was devised to find out if Venus could sometimes have earth-like conditions for a hot, anhydrous surfaces and zero-to-emitting air sluggish.
Scientists in the past have suggested that Venus's slow rotational speed is explained by its thick atmosphere.
But the latest simultigus suggests Venus could have had a thin, earth-like atmosphere, and could still have spiraled slowly.
The model also looked at the top formation of an early Venus.
The planet probably had areas drier than the Earth, but there was once enough water and geographical variability to enable life and to protect against rapid evaporation.
An early Venus also had the advantage of 30 per cent fainter sun.
"In the GISS model's simpligation of Venus, the slow rotation of Venus exposes its side of day to the sun for nearly two months at a time," GISS Researcher Anthony Del Genio explained in a news bulletin.
This heats the surface and generates the rain, which creates a thick layer of cloud, which acts, as it were, an umbrella to protect the surface of a large part of the sun's heat.
As a result, average climate temperatures are actually a few degrees cooler than Earth's current ones.
The model produced a tale of Venus's stages of development, similar to what planetary scientists had previously assumed.
While Venus was likely to be haphazard at times, it was exposed to excessive heat and ultra-violet light over several billion years.
Its oceans evacuated and its water vapors were burning under ultraviolet light.
When its hydrogen escaped, all that remained was a thick carbon dioxide-atmosphere with its deadly greenhouse gas phenomena, Venus that we know today.
Scholars shared the results of their new Venus climate model Geophysical Research Letters.
